
		Documentation for the Turing Machine simulator
	   ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
	This Turing machine simulator is an embedded language written in Forth. 
It defines the following (user level) words for Turing machine programming:
	state ( -- )
	      ( input stream: <name> )
	This creates a state variable in the vocabulary, which has no rules
	associated with it, and by default moves right across the tape
	left ( -- -1) 
	This is a movement constant
	right ( -- 1)
	Also a movement constant
	moves ( direction -- )
	this word, when used in conkunction with a direction, forces
	the state or rule defined immediately before it to move in the
	given direction
	-> ( -- )
	Semantically nil. I find that it makes turing machine code easier to
	read
	rule ( starting-state pattern-char replacement-char new-state -- )
	This produces a rule associated with the starting state. When the
	Turing machine is in this state, it will look for pattern-char in
	the "tape". When it finds this character it will replace it with
	replacement-char, and change state to new-state. By default, it
	will then move the tape in the direction start-state generally moves.
	However, a rule may have a different movement direction from its
	state's standard. This is done by following the word rule with the
	<movement-direction> moves phrase.
	halt ( -- nil )
	this is a constant state which signals the simulator to stop.
	tm-simulator ( starting-state -- )
		     ( input stream: tape-string for simulation)
	This is the loop about the tm-kernel which does the simulation.
	Using the Turing Machine simulator (a code example):
    This code figures out whether or not a string of X's is even or odd.
(code starts here)
\ Define states for counter
state even right moves
state odd right moves
\ Constants for pattern matching
ascii X constant X
ascii E constant E
ascii O constant O
ascii % constant %   ( turing machine standard notation for end of tape)
\ Rule tables
even X -> X odd rule	\ if we hit an X in even state, switch to odd state
odd X -> X even rule	\ if we hit an X in odd state, switch to even state
even % -> E halt rule	\ if we hit EOT in even state, say so and stop
odd % -> O halt rule	\ if we hit EOT in odd state, say so and stop
\ Simulation
even tm-simulator @XXXXYYXXYXYXYXbXXX%
(code ends)
Dave Weinstein
July 30, 1988


